A||||9||CUTTER||New break pad smoking.....normal?||||5||
Z||000000||CUTTER||05-25-2000||10:19 PM||||Today I got new break pads all the way around, and I drove to work and back with no problems. Then Later on I went to my friends house with one of my buddys following closly behind...and we all know you have to do a little more speedy driving to show off for your friend...I took a few turns pretty fast and used the breaks often, not going over 60mph. I then pulled into my friends driveway to notice smoke comming from my rear passenger side brake, puffs of it. It smelled like buring brake pad pretty bad. The smoke cleared up in a minute or two and that was the only one that smoked. We then went to the mall and came back 2 hours later I hoped in my 87GT and went home driving all speed limits and not breaking any laws. When I got home there was no smoke but I still smelt burning break pad a little just from that one tire. Is this normal for new break pads when they are abused for a short time untill they wear in or should I take it too the shop and tell them about it? Also when I was comming home I noticed a little squeaking sound comming from that tire. What could it be and what should I do?<BR>Thanks, Shane||||reg||5
Z||000001||lowCG||05-25-2000||10:38 PM||||Sounds like you might have a sticking caliper.<BR>New pads do need to outgas a little,or "bed-in" it's called.Usually a few few short,hard stops that get gradually more intense works best.||||reg||1
Z||000002||CUTTER||05-25-2000||10:46 PM||||If it is the caliper would I have to get another caliper or think it can be fixed?  Anyone else think they might know what it might be?<BR>Thanks, Shane||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fret Naught||05-26-2000||01:47 AM||||I sure don't think that a smoking brakepad is at all normal, done a lot of brake jobs and have never had one. I would look at a sticky Caliper or real dirty pins (the pins that alow the caliper to float) at any rate, if the caliper is bad a rebuild kit is pretty cheap and relitivly simple to do. My big concern would be how hot did it get? did it warp my rotor? are the pads glazed? If it were me, I would make sure that the caliper is floating. this allows the caliper and pads to release/re-center when the pedal is released,insure that there is no fluid leak, if there is a fluid leak then the caliper needs to be re-built. I would replace the pads on that side, and call it good. I would then take it easy for about 25 miles and watch it.<BR>Or, you could take it back to the place that did the brakes and have them look at it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||CUTTER||05-26-2000||02:00 AM||||would it be easy to check this myself? what do I look for? what should I watch for? if i need a rebuild kit where do I get one? how much and how easy?<BR>Thanks, Shane||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||05-26-2000||04:15 AM||||i guess you got a place to do your brakes?<P>What you would want to do is, first loosen the lug nuts on the wheel, get a jack and get your car up in the air and put some stands underneath the cradle. Take the nuts off the wheel, and pull the wheel off.  To remove the calipers, im pretty sure you need a torx bit.  Havn't done the brakes for a while.  Be careful, because if it slips, then you can strip the head.  <P>These are the steps to take a look at your brakes.  Just do what the previous member says, and look for things that don't look right.   ||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||05-26-2000||08:55 AM||||The caliper is sticking, whether it's the slider pins or the caliper itself, I don't know. Some shops get a bit sloppy about checking and lubing those slider pins. They require a special synthetic high temperature grease. You can take those pins out one at a time to check and lube them, pretty easy.<P>If it's the actual caliper, it's quicker and easier (a bit more expensive) to just install a new or rebuilt caliper. Seems like the rear Fiero calipers are more prone to fail than the front ones, in my experience.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 05-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||JAMIE||05-26-2000||08:55 AM||||I experienced exact same problem last year after I had brakes done - turned out to be a sticky caliper.Make sure you check your rotors to see if any damage done.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||05-26-2000||10:11 AM||||Do all of the years have sliders?  When the shop replaced my rear calipers, they had to heat up the old caliper to get the slider looking thing out.  I have no idea if they greased them or not.  Any suggestions???||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||05-26-2000||10:30 AM||||All Fieros have the "floating" calipers that are each held on with two slider pins/bolts. Not only should the pins be inspected for damaged and lubed, but the rubber o-rings inside the caliper that the pins slide on should be replaced frequently. Sometimes shops skimp on this, if the o-rings "look ok." In reality, the rubber gets hard and brittle, even if they "look ok."||||reg||1
